  <h1>Order-Book Reconstruction from Incremental Feeds</h1>
  <p class="why">Why this matters: this repo now processes a <strong>real</strong> Nasdaq ITCH day, and
  a single skipped message type silently corrupted the whole book — an 18-share phantom bid froze
  best-bid for six hours. This lesson is the post-mortem turned into theory.</p>
</header>

<h2>1 · The concept: a book is a fold over every event</h2>
<p>Exchanges don't broadcast the order book. They broadcast <strong>events</strong> — add this order,
execute that one, cancel part of another — and every consumer rebuilds the book by applying each
event, in order, to its own copy of the state. It's event sourcing at nanosecond scale:</p>
<pre><code>book_state(t) = fold(apply, empty_book, all events up to t)</code></pre>
<p>The property that makes this brutal: <strong>there is no error correction</strong>. Miss one event —
or handle one event type wrong — and your state diverges from the exchange's, permanently, with no
signal telling you so. Correctness is all-or-nothing over the <em>entire message taxonomy</em>:</p>

<div class="scrollbox"><table>
  <tr><th>ITCH type</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Book effect</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>'A'</code> / <code>'F'</code></td><td>Add order (anonymous / with MPID)</td><td>New resting order; depth += shares</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>'E'</code> / <code>'C'</code></td><td>Execute (at book / at other price)</td><td>Depth −= executed; order dies at 0</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>'X'</code></td><td><strong>Partial cancel</strong></td><td>Depth −= canceled shares</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>'D'</code></td><td>Delete</td><td>Order fully removed</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>'U'</code></td><td>Replace</td><td>Old order out, new order in (new ref/price/shares)</td></tr>
</table></div>

<p>Three of these five <em>remove</em> liquidity. Handling additions but missing any removal type means
your book only ever grows stale depth — and stale depth at an aggressive price becomes a phantom
best bid or ask that every downstream signal reads forever.</p>

<h2>2 · Minimal standalone example</h2>
<pre><code><span class="com"># A book is a fold; every event type must move the state or it rots.</span>
orders = {}                      <span class="com"># ref -> (side, price, shares)  (live orders)</span>
depth  = {}                      <span class="com"># (side, price) -> total shares (the book)</span>

<span class="kw">def</span> apply(event):
    kind = event[<span class="num">0</span>]
    <span class="kw">if</span> kind == <span class="str">"A"</span>:               <span class="com"># add: ref, side, price, shares</span>
        _, ref, side, px, sh = event
        orders[ref] = (side, px, sh)
        depth[(side, px)] = depth.get((side, px), <span class="num">0</span>) + sh
    <span class="kw">elif</span> kind <span class="kw">in</span> (<span class="str">"E"</span>, <span class="str">"X"</span>):     <span class="com"># execute OR cancel: both REMOVE shares</span>
        _, ref, sh = event
        side, px, left = orders[ref]
        take = min(sh, left)
        depth[(side, px)] -= take
        <span class="kw">if</span> left - take == <span class="num">0</span>: <span class="kw">del</span> orders[ref]
        <span class="kw">else</span>: orders[ref] = (side, px, left - take)
    <span class="com"># ...'D' and 'U' likewise. Comment out the "X" above and replay a real</span>
    <span class="com"># day: the book crosses within minutes and NOTHING tells you why.</span></code></pre>

<h2>3 · In this project: the phantom-depth post-mortem</h2>
<p>Until branch <code>feature/micro-3-signals-bridge</code>, <span class="path">cpp/itch/itch_parser.cpp</span>
listed <code>'X'</code> in its enum but routed it to the skip branch — harmless for the synthetic
benchmarks (which never send X), invisible in 24 green unit tests. Then the first <strong>real</strong>
AAPL day went through <code>MicrostructureEngine</code> and the top-of-book crossed on
<strong>100% of post-open events</strong>, with best-bid frozen at $289.75 × 18 shares.</p>
<p>Tracing that price level in the raw fixture found order <code>6129577</code>:</p>
<pre><code>A 350 sh @ 289.75   → E 199, X 17, X 1, E 2, E 131
executed 332 + canceled 18 = 350  → real order: GONE
our book (X skipped):  350 − 332 = 18 phantom shares, forever</code></pre>
<p>The fix (<span class="path">cpp/itch/itch_parser.hpp</span>, <span class="path">cpp/itch/microstructure_signals.hpp</span>):
a <code>CancelOrder</code> struct, an <code>on_cancel</code> visitor callback, and an engine handler that
reuses <code>book_.execute()</code> — a partial cancel and an execution are the <em>same state
transition</em> (reduce shares, drop at zero); they differ only in meaning (a trade happened vs. didn't).
After the fix: <strong>zero crossed events</strong> and a realistic 1–4¢ AAPL spread. The regression test
<code>MicroEngine.CancelViaParserRemovesPhantomDepth</code>
(<span class="path">cpp/tests/test_microstructure.cpp</span>) replays exactly this A→E→X pattern.</p>

<div class="callout good"><strong>Design lesson:</strong> the parser's paranoia counters
(<code>skipped_count</code>, <code>error_count</code>) made the bug <em>survivable</em> but not
<em>visible</em> — 431 skipped X messages looked identical to 431 skipped irrelevant messages.
Validation against reality (spread must be positive post-open) is what caught it. Synthetic tests
verify what you thought of; real data verifies what you didn't.</div>

<h2>4 · Often confused: incremental feed vs snapshot feed</h2>
<div class="scrollbox"><table>
  <tr><th></th><th>Incremental (ITCH, this repo)</th><th>Snapshot (e.g. REST "get order book")</th></tr>
  <tr><td>What arrives</td><td>Every individual event, order by order</td><td>The current top-N levels, on request</td></tr>
  <tr><td>State</td><td>You maintain it; exchange assumes you're perfect</td><td>Exchange maintains it; you just read</td></tr>
  <tr><td>A missed message</td><td>Permanent divergence, no signal</td><td>Self-heals on the next snapshot</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Latency / detail</td><td>Nanosecond, full depth, queue position</td><td>Delayed, truncated, no order identity</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Also confused:</td><td colspan="2"><code>'E'</code> execute vs <code>'X'</code> cancel vs <code>'D'</code> delete —
  identical <em>book</em> mechanics family (remove liquidity) but different <em>economics</em>: an E is a trade
  (prints, moves OFI via consumed depth), an X/D is liquidity withdrawn without trading.</td></tr>
</table></div>

