<div class="question" data-question>
  <p>Q1. Your reconstructed book shows a crossed top (bid ≥ ask) on nearly every event after the open. Most likely cause?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Exchange books can lock/cross pre-open, but the matching engine prevents persistent crossing in continuous trading — 'the market is weird' doesn't survive six hours.">The market was genuinely crossed that day</li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Persistent crossing means stale aggressive orders your state never removed — some removal path (X, D, E, or U-out) isn't being applied. Exactly the skipped-'X' bug this repo hit.">Your reconstruction is missing a removal message type, leaving phantom depth</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="An endianness bug garbles prices wildly and immediately (10000x errors), not a plausible-looking book that slowly rots.">A big-endian/little-endian mixup in the price field</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Out-of-range orders are rejected at add and counted — they never enter the book, so they can't sit there crossing it.">The book's declared price range is too wide</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p>Q2. Why could 24 passing unit tests coexist with a book-corrupting bug?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="The tests asserted plenty — every value was hand-computed. Coverage of inputs, not weakness of assertions, was the gap.">The tests didn't assert anything meaningful</li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Every test and benchmark fed the parser messages it handled. No synthetic input contained 'X', so the skip path never mattered — real data is what exercised the taxonomy you forgot.">All test inputs were synthetic and never contained the message type that was mishandled</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="ctest ran the suites fine; nothing was being skipped at the test-runner level.">The test runner was silently skipping the book tests</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="The engine wraps the book but both were tested; the bug was upstream in the parser's message routing, which was also tested — just never with an X.">The engine and the book were tested separately, so integration was untested</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p>Q3. ITCH order <code>ref=42</code>: A 100 sh, then E 60, then X 40. What does a correct book hold for ref 42?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="That ignores the partial cancel — the exact phantom-depth failure mode (100−60 = 40 stale shares).">40 shares still resting</li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="60 executed + 40 canceled = 100 = everything. The order is fully gone; depth at its price drops by 100 total and the ref is forgotten.">Nothing — the order is fully removed</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="X removes 40 more shares; only if X were skipped would anything remain, and it would be 40, not 60.">60 shares still resting</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="A 'D' is not required to finish an order — reaching zero via E/X removes it. Waiting for a D that never comes is exactly how state rots.">100 shares until a 'D' message arrives</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p>Q4. The engine's fix routes <code>on_cancel</code> through <code>book_.execute()</code>. Why is that legitimate rather than a hack?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Execute and partial-cancel are the identical state transition on the book (reduce shares, erase at zero); they differ in economic meaning (trade vs withdrawal), which the book doesn't model. Same mechanics ⇒ same code path.">Both events perform the same state transition; they differ only in economic meaning, which the book doesn't track</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="It's not about saving code — if the semantics differed (e.g. the book tracked traded volume), reuse would be wrong.">Because writing a separate cancel method would duplicate code</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="execute() already clamps to available shares and handles unknown refs via error_count — no special cancel-only safety was needed.">Because cancels need extra safety checks that execute() happens to have</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="ParseStats counts cancels separately (cancel_count); the stats layer distinguishes them fine.">Because cancels and executions must be counted together in the stats</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<h2>6 · Check your understanding</h2>
<p><strong>Scenario:</strong> You add support for a new venue whose feed has 31 message types; you need
only the 6 that move the book. A colleague says "parse those 6, skip the rest, ship it." What's the
one question you must answer about the other 25 before agreeing?
<em>(Answer: can any of them add, remove, or move liquidity — directly or by implication, like a
trading halt clearing the book? Every state-mutating type must be handled; only genuinely
state-neutral types may be skipped. And add a reality check — e.g. "book never stays crossed" — to
catch the ones you misjudged.)</em></p>

<script>
(function () {
  "use strict";
  var right = 0, answered = 0;
  var qs = document.querySelectorAll("[data-question]");
  function shuffle(a){for(var i=a.length-1;i>0;i--){var j=Math.floor(Math.random()*(i+1));var t=a[i];a[i]=a[j];a[j]=t;}return a;}
  qs.forEach(function (q) {
    var list = q.querySelector(".options");
    shuffle(Array.prototype.slice.call(list.children)).forEach(function(li){ list.appendChild(li); });
    var fb = q.querySelector(".feedback");
    var done = false;
    list.querySelectorAll("li").forEach(function (li) {
      li.addEventListener("click", function () {
        if (done) return;
        done = true; answered++;
        var ok = li.dataset.correct === "true";      // attribute, never position
        if (ok) right++;
        li.classList.add(ok ? "right" : "wrong");
        list.querySelectorAll("li").forEach(function (el) {
          el.classList.add("locked");
          if (el.dataset.correct === "true") el.classList.add("right");
        });
        fb.hidden = false;
        fb.textContent = (ok ? "✓ Correct. " : "✗ Not quite. ") + li.dataset.why;
        fb.style.color = ok ? "var(--ok)" : "var(--bad)";
        document.getElementById("score").textContent = right + " / " + qs.length;
      });
    });
  });
})();
</script>
